<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#romansh">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Romansh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Romansh language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Romansh is a Romance language spoken in the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, descended from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>. It has several regional varieties and a common written standard, Rumantsch Grischun, created in 1982. Recognised as a national language of Switzerland in 1938, it is spoken by about 60,000 people.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/romansh.jpg" alt="Romansh" width="320" height="314" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Terfili · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Romansh_language_composite.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
